---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Panel from '../components/Panel.astro';
import { TOOLS } from '../data/tools';

const tool = TOOLS.find((t) => t.href === '/theory')!;

const related = TOOLS.filter((t) =>
  ['/', '/sweep-generator', '/noise-generator', '/hearing-test', '/instrument-tuning', '/subwoofer-test'].includes(t.href),
);
---

<Base title={tool.title} description={tool.meta} tool={tool} keywords={tool.keywords}>
  <!-- ============================ Hero ============================ -->
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="hero__text">
      <p class="label label--accent">Reference · Physics of sound</p>
      <h1>Acoustic Theory</h1>
      <p class="hero__lead">
        A technical reference for the physics behind every generator on this site: what a sine wave is, how frequency,
        period and wavelength relate, why decibels are logarithmic, where harmonics come from, and how sampling,
        hearing and musical pitch work. Written for someone technical who is new to audio.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="hero__aside">
      <div class="notice">
        <span class="notice__icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span>
        <p class="notice__body">
          Several sections describe hearing thresholds and exposure limits. Read those before using the
          <a href="/hearing-test">hearing test</a> or a <a href="/sweep-generator">sweep generator</a>, and keep the
          site level low.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ======================= Calculator ======================= -->
  <section class="panel" id="calculator" aria-labelledby="calc-heading">
    <header class="panel__head">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="label" id="calc-heading">Calculator</span>
        <span class="badge">c = 343 m/s</span>
      </div>
      <span class="mono calc__formula">λ = c / f · T = 1 / f</span>
    </header>
    <div class="panel__body calc">
      <div class="calc__input">
        <label class="label" for="calc-freq">Frequency (Hz)</label>
        <input
          id="calc-freq"
          class="input input--readout mono"
          data-calc="freq"
          type="text"
          inputmode="decimal"
          value="440"
          autocomplete="off"
          spellcheck="false"
        />
        <p class="hint">
          Enter any value from 1 Hz to 20 kHz. The readouts update as you type. Wavelength uses c = 343 m/s, the speed
          of sound in dry air at 20 °C.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-grid calc__stats">
        <div class="stat"><p class="stat__k">Wavelength (m)</p><p class="stat__v" data-calc="wavelength-m">0.780 m</p></div>
        <div class="stat"><p class="stat__k">Wavelength (mm)</p><p class="stat__v" data-calc="wavelength-mm">780 mm</p></div>
        <div class="stat"><p class="stat__k">Period (ms)</p><p class="stat__v" data-calc="period-ms">2.27 ms</p></div>
        <div class="stat"><p class="stat__k">Nearest note</p><p class="stat__v" data-calc="nearest">A4 · 440 Hz</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== Long-form reference ===================== -->
  <Panel eyebrow="Reference" title="Acoustic theory">
    <div class="prose-tech">
      <h2>1. What a sine wave is</h2>
      <p>
        A sine wave is the simplest possible sound. At any moment its value is
      </p>
      <p><code>y = A · sin(2πft + φ)</code></p>
      <p>
        where <code>A</code> is the amplitude, <code>f</code> is the frequency in hertz, <code>t</code> is time in
        seconds and <code>φ</code> is the phase in radians. Amplitude sets how far the wave swings from zero, which
        you hear as level. Frequency sets how many cycles pass each second, which you hear as pitch. Phase sets where
        in its cycle the wave starts, which you hear only when two waves combine.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cycle repeats every period <code>T = 1 / f</code>. A 440 Hz tone repeats 440 times a second, so one period
        lasts <code>1 / 440 ≈ 2.273 ms</code>.
      </p>
      <h3>Why sine is the building block</h3>
      <p>
        The Fourier theorem states that any repeating signal, however complex, can be written as a sum of sine waves at
        whole-number multiples of a base frequency. The reverse is also true: a complex sound is nothing but a set of
        sines added together. This is why a sine is the reference signal for calibration and testing. It contains
        energy at exactly one frequency and nothing else, so anything the sine picks up is a property of the system
        under test, not of the signal.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Frequency, period and wavelength</h2>
      <p>
        Frequency and period are reciprocal:
      </p>
      <p><code>f = 1 / T</code></p>
      <p>
        Wavelength is the physical distance the wave travels through one cycle:
      </p>
      <p><code>λ = c / f</code></p>
      <p>
        where <code>c</code> is the speed of sound. In dry air at 20 °C, <code>c ≈ 343 m/s</code>. The speed is not
        constant. It rises with temperature at roughly <code>0.606 m/s</code> per °C, so a rough model is
        <code>c ≈ 331.3 + 0.606 · θ</code> m/s for air temperature <code>θ</code> in °C. At 0 °C the speed is about
        331 m/s; at 30 °C it is about 349 m/s. Humidity changes it by a smaller amount. All wavelengths in this
        reference use 343 m/s.
      </p>
      <table class="table-tech">
        <caption class="label">Period and wavelength at reference frequencies (c = 343 m/s)</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Frequency</th><th>Period</th><th>Wavelength (m)</th><th>Wavelength (mm)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">20 Hz</td><td class="mono">50 ms</td><td class="mono">17.15 m</td><td class="mono">17 150 mm</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">100 Hz</td><td class="mono">10 ms</td><td class="mono">3.43 m</td><td class="mono">3 430 mm</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">440 Hz</td><td class="mono">2.273 ms</td><td class="mono">0.780 m</td><td class="mono">780 mm</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">1 kHz</td><td class="mono">1 ms</td><td class="mono">0.343 m</td><td class="mono">343 mm</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">10 kHz</td><td class="mono">0.1 ms</td><td class="mono">0.0343 m</td><td class="mono">34.3 mm</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">20 kHz</td><td class="mono">0.05 ms</td><td class="mono">0.01715 m</td><td class="mono">17.15 mm</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The pattern to remember: raise the frequency by a factor of ten and the wavelength drops by the same factor. A
        20 Hz wave is longer than a room. A 20 kHz wave is smaller than a fingertip.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Amplitude and the decibel</h2>
      <p>
        Human hearing spans an enormous range of pressures, from the threshold of hearing to the threshold of pain. A
        linear scale would be unusable, so level is measured in decibels, which are logarithmic.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a ratio of two powers, <code>dB = 10 · log10(P2 / P1)</code>. For a ratio of two voltages or amplitudes,
        <code>dB = 20 · log10(A2 / A1)</code>. The factor of two appears because power is proportional to amplitude
        squared.
      </p>
      <h3>The numbers worth memorising</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><code>6 dB</code> is a doubling of voltage or amplitude, <code>20 · log10(2) ≈ 6.02 dB</code>.</li>
        <li><code>10 dB</code> is a doubling of perceived loudness, a rule of thumb that holds over the mid range.</li>
        <li><code>3 dB</code> is a doubling of power, <code>10 · log10(2) ≈ 3.01 dB</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>The three decibel scales used here</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>dB SPL</strong> is sound pressure level relative to 20 µPa, the nominal threshold of hearing. It is an
        absolute measure of the acoustic pressure in air. A quiet room is about 30 dB SPL; normal speech is about
        60 dB SPL.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>dBFS</strong> is decibels relative to digital full scale. 0 dBFS is the largest value a sample can
        hold. Everything below it is negative. A signal at -6 dBFS peaks at half the amplitude of full scale. Digital
        clipping happens when a value tries to exceed 0 dBFS.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>dBu</strong> is decibels relative to 0.775 V, a reference inherited from telephone and line-level
        equipment. It describes an electrical voltage, not sound pressure, so it is only meaningful for the analogue
        part of a chain.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Harmonics and timbre</h2>
      <p>
        A pure sine is one frequency. A real instrument radiates many frequencies at once, and the mix of those
        frequencies is what we call timbre. The components above the lowest one, the fundamental, are called
        harmonics, at whole-number multiples of the fundamental.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each classic waveform has a fixed recipe of harmonics:
      </p>
      <table class="table-tech">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Waveform</th><th>Harmonics present</th><th>Amplitude of harmonic n</th><th>Character</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">Square</td><td class="mono">Odd only</td><td class="mono">1 / n</td><td>Hollow and buzzy</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">Sawtooth</td><td class="mono">All harmonics</td><td class="mono">1 / n</td><td>Bright and string-like</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">Triangle</td><td class="mono">Odd only</td><td class="mono">1 / n²</td><td>Mellow and soft</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        A square wave keeps only the odd harmonics, at 1, 3, 5, 7 and so on, each with amplitude <code>1 / n</code>. A
        sawtooth keeps every harmonic, also at <code>1 / n</code>, so it has roughly twice the number of partials and
        sounds brighter. A triangle keeps only the odd harmonics but rolls them off as <code>1 / n²</code>, so the high
        partials fade much faster and the result is softer than a square.
      </p>
      <p>
        A square and a sawtooth sound bright because their high harmonics stay strong as <code>1 / n</code>, which
        falls slowly. A sine has no harmonics at all, and a triangle loses them as <code>1 / n²</code>, which falls
        fast. This is also why a square or saw reads as a dense comb of peaks in a spectrum display, while a sine reads
        as a single peak.
      </p>
      <h3>Duty cycle</h3>
      <p>
        A square wave with equal high and low halves has a 50 % duty cycle. Change the fraction of the period spent
        high and the harmonic content changes: a pulse narrower than 50 % develops even harmonics, and the timbre moves
        from hollow towards thinner and more nasal. The pulse train in the <a href="/alarm-generator">alarm
        generator</a> is the audible version of this.
      </p>
      <h3>Aliasing of harmonics above Nyquist</h3>
      <p>
        Harmonics do not stop at the audible limit. A 1 kHz square has partials at 3, 5, 7 kHz and onward forever. In
        a digital system, any harmonic above half the sample rate, the Nyquist frequency, cannot be represented. It
        does not simply disappear. It folds back to a lower frequency, a mirror image of itself, and appears as an
        inharmonic tone that was not in the original signal. Good synthesisers limit the number of harmonics generated
        so that none cross Nyquist, which is why a high square wave in a digital generator can sound less rich than
        the maths suggests.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Sampling</h2>
      <p>
        Digital audio stores a sound as a series of measurements, or samples, taken at a steady rate, the sample rate.
        CD audio uses 44 100 samples per second, written <code>44.1 kHz</code>. The rate must be high enough to capture
        the fastest change in the signal.
      </p>
      <h3>The Nyquist–Shannon theorem</h3>
      <p>
        The sampling theorem states that a signal can be reconstructed exactly only if the sample rate is greater than
        twice the highest frequency present:
      </p>
      <p><code>fs &gt; 2 · fmax</code></p>
      <p>
        Half the sample rate is the Nyquist frequency. At 44.1 kHz it is 22.05 kHz. Any component above it folds back
        and is misrepresented, so a real converter filters the input to remove energy above Nyquist before sampling.
      </p>
      <h3>Why 44.1 kHz</h3>
      <p>
        44.1 kHz was chosen for the compact disc for two reasons. The audible range tops out at roughly 20 kHz, so the
        rate must exceed 40 kHz. The extra 4.1 kHz gives the anti-aliasing filter a transition band: a filter cannot
        cut off instantly, so the standard places the passband edge at 20 kHz and the stopband by 22.05 kHz, leaving a
        small gap to roll off in. Many hardware converters run at 48 kHz, which leaves slightly more headroom.
      </p>
      <h3>Quantisation and dynamic range</h3>
      <p>
        Each sample is stored with a fixed number of bits. 16-bit audio has 65 536 possible values. The gap between
        adjacent values is the quantisation step, and the error this introduces sets the noise floor. Every extra bit
        adds about 6 dB of dynamic range, so 16 bits give roughly <code>16 · 6.02 ≈ 96 dB</code> between the loudest
        signal and the noise floor.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Dithering</strong> is the practice of adding a tiny amount of low-level noise before quantisation. The
        error from rounding a sample is correlated with the signal and sounds like harsh distortion on quiet passages.
        Adding a trace of noise decorrelates that error and turns it into a gentle hiss, which the ear tolerates far
        better. The noise is below -90 dBFS in 16-bit audio and is inaudible in most material.
      </p>

      <h2>6. How humans hear</h2>
      <p>
        A healthy young ear covers roughly 20 Hz to 20 kHz. The upper limit falls with age and with noise exposure, and
        the drop is gradual rather than a hard edge.
      </p>
      <table class="table-tech">
        <caption class="label">Typical upper frequency limit by age</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Age</th><th>Typical upper limit</th><th>Comment</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">Under 20</td><td class="mono">20 kHz</td><td>Best case, undamaged hearing</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">20 to 29</td><td class="mono">17 kHz</td><td>Slow loss begins</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">30 to 39</td><td class="mono">16 kHz</td><td>High frequencies recede</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">40 to 49</td><td class="mono">14 kHz</td><td>Common point at which 15 kHz is lost</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">50 to 59</td><td class="mono">12 kHz</td><td>Presbycusis is clear</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">60 and over</td><td class="mono">10 kHz</td><td>Highly variable between people</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        These are typical values, not rules. Noise exposure, genetics and medical history move them a long way in
        either direction.
      </p>
      <h3>Loudness is not linear</h3>
      <p>
        The ear does not treat all frequencies as equally loud. Equal-loudness contours, first mapped by Fletcher and
        Munson and now standardised as ISO 226, show how much level each frequency needs to sound as loud as a
        reference tone. The curves bunch up near 3 to 4 kHz, where the ear canal resonates and hearing is most
        sensitive. This is why a quiet 3 kHz tone can seem far louder than a quiet 100 Hz tone at the same measured
        level, and why mixing by SPL alone is not enough.
      </p>
      <p>
        As level rises the contours flatten: quiet sounds seem to lose their low and high end, loud sounds seem fuller.
        This is the basis of the loudness control on older amplifiers, and the reason a mix judged at low volume does
        not translate directly to a loud system.
      </p>
      <h3>Masking</h3>
      <p>
        A loud sound hides a quieter one nearby in frequency. This is masking, and it is why white or pink noise can
        cover a conversation in an open office. Masking is strongest for frequencies just above the masker, and it
        spreads with the masker's level. Lossy codecs exploit it by discarding components that a nearby strong tone
        would hide anyway. The <a href="/noise-generator">noise generator</a> is a practical masking tool.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Musical pitch</h2>
      <p>
        Modern Western instruments use twelve-tone equal temperament: the octave is divided into twelve equal steps, and
        each step is a frequency ratio of <code>2^(1/12) ≈ 1.05946</code>. Equal steps mean instruments can play in any
        key without retuning, at the cost of every interval except the octave being slightly impure.
      </p>
      <p>
        The size of an interval in cents is
      </p>
      <p><code>cents = 1200 · log2(f2 / f1)</code></p>
      <p>
        One semitone is 100 cents; one octave is 1200 cents. Concert pitch sets A4 at 440 Hz, defined by ISO 16. The
        frequency of any equal-tempered note from its MIDI number <code>n</code>, where A4 is 69, is
      </p>
      <p><code>f = 440 · 2^((n − 69) / 12)</code></p>
      <p>
        Alternative references are common. Verdi tuning puts A4 at 432 Hz, about 31.8 cents flat of concert pitch:
        <code>1200 · log2(432 / 440) ≈ -31.8</code> cents. Baroque pitch uses 415 Hz. The difference is audible when
        the two references are played back to back, but within a single piece it changes the character of the
        instruments, not the intervals. The <a href="/432hz">432 Hz page</a> plays each reference directly.
      </p>

      <h2>8. Beats and interference</h2>
      <p>
        Two tones close in frequency and played together rise and fall in level at a rate equal to the difference
        between them:
      </p>
      <p><code>f_beat = |f1 − f2|</code></p>
      <p>
        A 440 Hz tone against a 444 Hz tone produces a 4 Hz beat, four pulses a second. The effect is strongest below
        about 20 beats per second, after which the two tones merge into a single rough or dissonant sound. Beats are
        the standard way to tune an instrument by ear: remove the beating and the pitches match. The
        <a href="/multiple-tone-generator">multiple tone generator</a> can play two tones at once so you can hear this
        directly.
      </p>
      <h3>Binaural and monaural beats</h3>
      <p>
        A monaural beat occurs when both tones reach both ears and mix in the air or in one channel. A binaural beat
        occurs when each ear receives only one tone, through headphones. The difference frequency is then created in
        the brain rather than in the room, and it can be perceived even though no single place in the air contains the
        beat. Binaural beats require stereo separation, which the
        <a href="/binaural-beats">binaural beat generator</a> provides. Claims about their effect on brain states are
        not firmly established, and this site presents them as tones to listen to, not as therapy.
      </p>
      <h3>Standing waves and room modes</h3>
      <p>
        Sound reflects from walls, and a reflected wave interferes with the incoming one. At frequencies where the room
        dimension is a multiple of half a wavelength, the reflections reinforce into a standing wave: some positions
        get louder, others quieter, and the pressure swells at particular frequencies. These are room modes, given for
        a dimension <code>L</code> by
      </p>
      <p><code>f = c / (2 · L)</code></p>
      <p>
        for the fundamental, and integer multiples of it for the higher modes. In a room 4 m long, the first axial mode
        is <code>343 / (2 · 4) ≈ 42.9 Hz</code>, with the next at 85.8 Hz, 128.6 Hz and so on. A tone near one of those
        frequencies can sound uneven as you move around the room. The
        <a href="/subwoofer-test">subwoofer test</a> exists to find these by ear.
      </p>

      <h2>9. Hearing safety</h2>
      <p>
        Sound exposure damages hearing by dose, not by peak alone. Damage accumulates from level multiplied by time,
        and it is permanent. Recognised occupational limits use a 3 dB exchange rate: every 3 dB of extra level halves
        the safe exposure time.
      </p>
      <table class="table-tech">
        <caption class="label">Typical exposure limits, 3 dB exchange rate</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Level</th><th>Recommended maximum</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">80 dB</td><td class="mono">40 hours per week</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">85 dB</td><td class="mono">8 hours per day</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">91 dB</td><td class="mono">2 hours</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">94 dB</td><td class="mono">1 hour</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">100 dB</td><td class="mono">15 minutes</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">110 dB</td><td class="mono">Under 2 minutes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        These values follow WHO and NIOSH guidance for occupational noise. A pure tone concentrates its energy at one
        place on the cochlea, and long exposure to a single frequency is harder on that spot than the same level of
        broadband sound. Tones are also unforgiving of concentration: a sustained 2 kHz tone is far more fatiguing than
        speech at the same level.
      </p>
      <h3>Protecting yourself while testing</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Start with the site level low and raise it only as far as you need to hear.</li>
        <li>Keep test tones short. A few seconds per frequency is enough to judge audibility.</li>
        <li>Never test tones in headphones at high level to "check" a frequency. Use a level you would accept for a
          whole album.</li>
        <li>Take breaks. Hearing fatigue dulls sensitivity and makes judgements unreliable.</li>
        <li>If a tone causes discomfort, ringing or a sense of pressure, stop immediately.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This site cannot measure the sound pressure reaching your ears. The level slider on this site controls a
        digital gain, and the true SPL depends on your device, headphones and the setting of your system volume. Treat
        the limits above as a reason for caution, not as a calibrated measurement.
      </p>

      <h2>10. Reference frequencies measured on this site</h2>
      <p>
        The table below lists the fixed frequencies the tools on this site use. They come from the standards named at
        the end of the page, not from estimation.
      </p>
      <table class="table-tech">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Frequency</th><th>Where it is used</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">50 Hz</td><td>Mains hum in Europe, and most of the world</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">60 Hz</td><td>Mains hum in North America and parts of Asia</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">300 to 3400 Hz</td><td>Speech band of a telephone channel, the classic POTS band</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">697 to 1633 Hz</td><td>DTMF keypad tones: rows 697, 770, 852, 941 Hz; columns 1209, 1336, 1477, 1633 Hz</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">1000 Hz</td><td>The pips time signal, six 1000 Hz pips on the hour</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">250 Hz to 8 kHz</td><td>Standard audiometric test frequencies for a hearing test</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">17.4 kHz</td><td>The "mosquito" tone, chosen to be audible to younger ears</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">20 kHz</td><td>Nominal upper limit of human hearing, and the top of this site's range</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </Panel>

  <!-- ============================ Sources ============================ -->
  <Panel eyebrow="References" title="Standards this page cites">
    <div class="prose-tech">
      <p>Plain-text list of the standards and results referenced above.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>ISO 16</strong>: standard musical pitch, A4 = 440 Hz.</li>
        <li><strong>ISO 226</strong>: normal equal-loudness-level contours (the Fletcher and Munson work, later revised).</li>
        <li><strong>IEC 61672</strong>: sound level meters and the definition of the weighting scales behind dB SPL.</li>
        <li><strong>Nyquist–Shannon sampling theorem</strong>: exact reconstruction requires a sample rate above twice the highest frequency present.</li>
        <li><strong>WHO and NIOSH</strong>: occupational noise exposure limits and the 3 dB exchange rate.</li>
        <li><strong>ITU-T Q.23 / Q.24</strong>: the DTMF tone plan and signalling frequencies.</li>
        <li><strong>IEC 60645</strong>: audiometric equipment, including the standard test frequencies.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Panel>

  <!-- ===================== Related tools ===================== -->
  <Panel eyebrow="More tools" title="Related generators and tests">
    <div class="card-grid">
      {
        related.map((item) => (
          <a class="card" href={item.href}>
            <p class="card__t">{item.title}</p>
            <p class="card__d">{item.tagline}</p>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </Panel>

  <script>
    import '../scripts/theory';
  </script>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.5fr) minmax(260px, 1fr);
    gap: 20px;
    align-items: start;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .hero { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }
  .hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.2vw, 2.1rem);
    margin: 6px 0 8px;
  }
  .hero__lead {
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
    max-width: 68ch;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 14.5px;
  }
  .notice__body { margin: 0; }

  .calc {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.85fr) minmax(0, 1.4fr);
    gap: 16px;
    align-items: start;
  }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .calc { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
